    "question": {
      ":question/id": "6594bfee-91ce-5b8b-bd3e-19a5034ddb1b",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Let $p=-5i-6j+xk$ and $q=6i-2j-3k.$ Given that the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$ is zero, find the value of $x.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot 6+(-6)\\cdot (-2)+x\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-18-3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ -18-3x & =0 \\\\ 3x & =-18 \\\\ x & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
