      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Let $p=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ and $q=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ Given that the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$ is $0,$ find the value of $x.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot x+6\\cdot 2+(-4)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-5x+16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ -5x+16 & =0 \\\\ 5x & =16 \\\\ x & =\\frac{16}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
